# tsundoku — copy to `.env.local` and adjust. `.env.local` is gitignored.
#
# Nothing is hosted at this stage. The whole local loop runs on a Postgres in
# Docker that `pnpm db:up` creates, and there is no Google OAuth client to make,
# no bearer token to obtain and no cloud account to open. Going public is a later
# step (ADR-0003, ADR-0004).

# ── The one variable, and the only one ────────────────────────────────────────
# `DATABASE_URL` carries the host, the port, the credentials and the database
# name, and everything reads it: `next dev`, `pnpm db:*` and `pnpm test`. It is
# also what the container is built from, so changing the port here is the whole
# change — `pnpm db:up` publishes whatever port this names and names the
# container after it (`tsundoku-pg` on 5432, `tsundoku-pg-5433` on 5433), so two
# checkouts on different ports never collide.
#
# Change it if something already listens on 5432 — a Postgres installed on the
# host, most often — or if a second checkout needs its own database. `pnpm test`
# uses the same server and the same name with `_test` appended, which it creates.
#
# A value set in the shell wins over this file, so `DATABASE_URL=… pnpm test`
# works without editing anything.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://tsundoku:tsundoku@127.0.0.1:5432/tsundoku

# ── The owner gate ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The web view is gated by Google, restricted to one address (ADR-0004). **None of
# the four variables that boot the real gate carries a value in this file and none
# ever should**: three of them are secrets, and the fourth is a person. The one
# variable set below is not one of them — it is the local opt-in that stands in for
# the gate while there is nothing to sign in to.
#
# **Nothing is hosted at this stage, so there is no Google OAuth client yet.** The
# local loop therefore runs with the gate open, by an act rather than by the absence
# of a configuration:
#
#   AUTH_DEV_OPEN=true
#
# It is never honoured in a production build — the container runs `next start` —
# so it cannot be the reason the library ends up readable from the internet. With
# it set, Auth.js is not reached at all: no client id, no secret, no `AUTH_SECRET`.
AUTH_DEV_OPEN=true

# The four variables that boot the real gate, once there is a Google OAuth client
# to point at. Leave them unset until then; the gate fails closed without them, and
# a deployment missing one refuses the owner rather than admitting everybody.
#
#   AUTH_SECRET          what the session cookie is signed and encrypted with.
#                        `pnpm dlx auth secret` prints one. Rotating it is the kill
#                        switch: it invalidates every session at once.
#   AUTH_GOOGLE_ID       the OAuth client id from Google Cloud Console.
#   AUTH_GOOGLE_SECRET   its secret.
#   AUTH_OWNER_EMAIL     the one Google address that may sign in. Singular: a
#                        comma in this value is a misconfiguration, not a list, and
#                        the gate refuses it rather than admitting the first name.
#
# The authorised redirect URI to register with Google is
# `https://<domain>/api/auth/callback/google`, and `http://localhost:3000/api/auth/callback/google`
# if you want to exercise the real gate locally.
#
# AUTH_SECRET=
# AUTH_GOOGLE_ID=
# AUTH_GOOGLE_SECRET=
# AUTH_OWNER_EMAIL=you@example.com

# ── The MCP door's bearer token ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# `/mcp` is the other door and is authenticated by a **static bearer token**
# rather than by Google (ADR-0004). One string is enough, and there is no
# authorization server and nothing to register; `src/lib/mcp/README.md` has the
# argument and the primary sources behind it.
#
# **It carries no value in this file and never should.** It is a secret, and it
# is the only thing standing in front of the library on that door.
#
# The gate fails closed: unset or blank, `/mcp` refuses every request. There is
# no development opt-in beside it, unlike `AUTH_DEV_OPEN`, because this is a
# string you pick. To try the door locally, put any string in your `.env.local`:
#
#   MCP_BEARER_TOKEN=$(openssl rand -hex 32)
#
# Rotating it disconnects every assistant at once, which is the only kill switch
# this door has.
#
# MCP_BEARER_TOKEN=

# ── Who this copy says it is, to the cover sources ────────────────────────────
# The cover lookup asks Google Books and Open Library over HTTP, and it signs every
# request with a user-agent naming an address a human can be reached at. Open
# Library gives an identified caller three requests a second where an anonymous one
# gets one, so the header is never omitted — only pointed somewhere true.
#
# **Not a secret, and not a duplicate of `AUTH_URL`.** That one has to match the
# redirect URI registered with Google exactly; this one is a contact, and a copy
# running on a LAN with no public origin at all has the second and not the first.
#
# Unset — which is the whole local loop, where the lookup is run by hand a few times
# — the app identifies itself as the project, `https://github.com/davideimola/tsundoku`,
# rather than as anybody's deployment. Set it when you host this somewhere, so the
# complaint about your traffic reaches you and not the original.
#
# COVER_CONTACT_URL=https://library.example.com

# ── The API door's bearer token ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# `/api` is the third door and it is read-only by construction (ADR-0025). Its
# first resource is `GET /api/showcase`, one document a public page renders:
# what is being read or played, what concluded, what is unopened, and a sample of
# the shelf. Prices, acquisitions, the Inbox, Paths and the prose of a Rating are
# not in it and cannot be asked for.
#
# **It carries no value in this file and never should.** It is a secret, and it
# is the only thing standing in front of that door.
#
# It is deliberately **not** `MCP_BEARER_TOKEN`. That one lives in the owner's own
# assistant; this one lives in a third party's environment (the host that builds
# the page), so it has to be rotatable there without reconnecting an assistant and
# revocable here without touching one.
#
# The gate fails closed: unset or blank, every route under `/api` refuses. There
# is no development opt-in beside it, for `MCP_BEARER_TOKEN`'s reason. To try the
# door locally, put any string in your `.env.local`:
#
#   API_BEARER_TOKEN=$(openssl rand -hex 32)
#
# API_BEARER_TOKEN=

# ── Whether that door publishes a wishlist ────────────────────────────────────
# Off unless this says `true`, and off is the right answer for this library: what
# the owner means to buy is a plan rather than a library. A fork that wants a
# public wishlist turns it on, and even then the block carries titles and tiles
# and none of the four numbers the shopping list is made of.
#
# SHOWCASE_WISHLIST=true
